<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>During the preparation of this article, S.C. was partially supported by NSF RTG grant DMS-1502651. H.L. was supported by the Hertz Foundation and NSF GRFP under grant DGE-1656518. This work will be part of S.C.’s and H.L.’s Ph.D. theses.</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Samir Canning
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Department of Mathematics, ETH Zürich, Rämistrasse 101, 8092 Zürich, Switzerland.
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hannah Larson
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Department of Mathematics, Harvard University, One Oxford Street, Cambridge, MA 02138.
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:samir.canning@math.ethz.ch">samir.canning@math.ethz.ch</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:hlarson@math.harvard.edu">hlarson@math.harvard.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The rational Chow ring of the moduli space <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> of curves of genus <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is known for <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, we determine the rational Chow rings of <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7},\mathcal{M}_{8}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7},\mathcal{M}_{8},</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{9}</annotation></semantics></math> by showing they are tautological.
The key ingredient is intersection theory on Hurwitz spaces of degree <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> covers of <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> via their associated vector bundles. The main focus of this paper is a detailed geometric analysis of special tetragonal and pentagonal covers whose associated vector bundles on <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are highly unbalanced, expanding upon previous work of the authors in the more balanced case. In genus <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>, we use work of Mukai to present the locus of hexagonal curves as a global quotient stack,
and, using equivariant intersection theory, we show its Chow ring is generated by restrictions of tautological classes.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In his landmark paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, Mumford introduced the Chow ring of the moduli space <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves. Since then, much progress has been made on the determination of <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> in low genus, which we summarize below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<math id="S1.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mumford <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> in 1983, determined <math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with rational coefficients. 
<br class="ltx_break">Vistoli <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> in 1998, determined <math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with integral coefficients, 
<br class="ltx_break">E. Larson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> in 2020, determined <math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with integral coefficients.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<math id="S1.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> in 1990, determined <math id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> with rational coefficients, 
<br class="ltx_break">Di Lorenzo–Fulghesu–Vistoli <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> in 2020, determined the integral Chow ring of the locus of smooth plane quartics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<math id="S1.I1.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> in 1990, determined <math id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{4})</annotation></semantics></math> with rational coefficients.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<math id="S1.I1.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Izadi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> in 1995, determined <math id="S1.I1.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{5})</annotation></semantics></math> with rational coefficients.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<math id="S1.I1.ix5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=6</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Penev–Vakil <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> in 2015, determined <math id="S1.I1.ix5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{6})</annotation></semantics></math> with rational coefficients.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In each of the above cases,
the rational Chow ring of <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tautological subring</em> <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})\subseteq A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})\subseteq A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, a subring generated by certain natural classes which we now define.
Let <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{C}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{C}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal curve.
The tautological subring is the subring of <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">kappa classes</em>, <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{i}:=f_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{f})^{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{i}:=f_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{f})^{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we
tackle the next open cases of genus <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7,8</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> using the new machinery of tautological classes on the Hurwitz space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. We prove that the rational Chow rings of <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7},\mathcal{M}_{8}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7},\mathcal{M}_{8},</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{9}</annotation></semantics></math> are all generated by tautological classes, and thereby determine these Chow rings using work of Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.
In addition to our theorems in genus <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7,8</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>, our techniques give new and much simpler proofs of the genus <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> cases (see Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Genus 
          
            5
          
         and 
          
            6 ‣ 4. The Tetragonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).
In particular, in genus <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>, we establish that all classes supported on the
bielliptic locus are tautological, which was not fully explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Chow ring of the moduli space of genus <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> curves is generated by tautological classes. Hence,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{7})\cong\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}]/I_{7}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{7})\cong\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}]/I_{7},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ideal generated by the classes</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}2423\kappa_{1}^{2}\kappa_{2}-52632\kappa_{2}^{2}\\
1152000\kappa_{2}^{2}-2423\kappa_{1}^{4}\\
16000\kappa_{1}^{3}\kappa_{2}-731\kappa_{1}^{5}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>2423</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>52632</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1152000</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2423</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>16000</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>731</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>5</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}2423\kappa_{1}^{2}\kappa_{2}-52632\kappa_{2}^{2}\\
1152000\kappa_{2}^{2}-2423\kappa_{1}^{4}\\
16000\kappa_{1}^{3}\kappa_{2}-731\kappa_{1}^{5}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The computation of the tautological ring is originally due to Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. We used the Sage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> package admcycles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and a program of Pixton <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> to obtain the above presentation and those below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Chow ring of the moduli space of genus <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> curves is generated by tautological classes. Hence,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{8})=\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}]/I_{8}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{8})=\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}]/I_{8},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{8}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ideal generated by the classes</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}714894336\kappa_{2}^{2}-55211328\kappa_{1}^{2}\kappa_{2}+1058587\kappa_{1}^{4}\\
62208000\kappa_{1}\kappa_{2}^{2}-95287\kappa_{1}^{5}\\
144000\kappa_{1}^{3}\kappa_{2}-5617\kappa_{1}^{5}.\\
\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>714894336</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>55211328</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1058587</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>62208000</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>95287</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>5</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>144000</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>5617</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>5</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}714894336\kappa_{2}^{2}-55211328\kappa_{1}^{2}\kappa_{2}+1058587\kappa_{1}^{4}\\
62208000\kappa_{1}\kappa_{2}^{2}-95287\kappa_{1}^{5}\\
144000\kappa_{1}^{3}\kappa_{2}-5617\kappa_{1}^{5}.\\
\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The authors would like to point out contemporaneous work of Maxwell da Paixão de Jesus Santos, which, using different techniques, makes significant progress towards showing <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{8})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{8})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological (it is proved that non-tautological classes must be supported on the bielliptic locus).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Chow ring of the moduli space of genus <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> curves is generated by tautological classes. Hence,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{9})=\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\kappa_{3}]/I_{9}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{9})=\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\kappa_{3}]/I_{9},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{9}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ideal generated by the classes</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}5195\kappa_{1}^{4}+3644694\kappa_{1}\kappa_{3}+749412\kappa_{2}^{2}-265788\kappa_{1}^{2}\kappa_{2}\\
33859814400\kappa_{2}\kappa_{3}-95311440\kappa_{1}^{3}\kappa_{2}+2288539\kappa_{1}^{5}\\
19151377\kappa_{1}^{5}+16929907200\kappa_{1}\kappa_{2}^{2}-1142345520\kappa_{1}^{3}\kappa_{2}\\
1422489600\kappa_{3}^{2}-983\kappa_{1}^{6}\\
1185408000\kappa_{2}^{3}-47543\kappa_{1}^{6}.\\
\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5195</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3644694</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>749412</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>265788</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>33859814400</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>95311440</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2288539</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>5</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>19151377</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>5</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16929907200</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1142345520</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1422489600</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>983</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1185408000</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>47543</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}5195\kappa_{1}^{4}+3644694\kappa_{1}\kappa_{3}+749412\kappa_{2}^{2}-265788\kappa_{1}^{2}\kappa_{2}\\
33859814400\kappa_{2}\kappa_{3}-95311440\kappa_{1}^{3}\kappa_{2}+2288539\kappa_{1}^{5}\\
19151377\kappa_{1}^{5}+16929907200\kappa_{1}\kappa_{2}^{2}-1142345520\kappa_{1}^{3}\kappa_{2}\\
1422489600\kappa_{3}^{2}-983\kappa_{1}^{6}\\
1185408000\kappa_{2}^{3}-47543\kappa_{1}^{6}.\\
\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite their complicated looking presentations, the tautological rings above have many nice properties. Faber proved that they are Gorenstein rings with socle in degree <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-2</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. He also points out that <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=9</annotation></semantics></math> is the first case in which the tautological ring is not a complete intersection ring.
Several different methods of producing relations among tautological classes in arbitrary genus have found only the Faber–Zagier relations, which may suggest that the Gorenstein property only occurs in low genus cases (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> for a discussion).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An interesting consequence of Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>, and <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> is that for <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=7,8,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=7,8,9</annotation></semantics></math>, the cycle class map <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})\rightarrow H^{2*}(\mathcal{M}_{g},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})\rightarrow H^{2*}(\mathcal{M}_{g},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is injective. It is unknown whether this holds in general, and it could even fail quite dramatically: when <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is large, it is unknown whether <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite or infinite dimensional as a <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space, whereas cohomology is finitely generated for any algebraic variety. On the moduli space of stable curves, Pikaart <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, Corollary 4.7]</cite> has shown that <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g},\mathbb{Q})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>33</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g},\mathbb{Q})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large. It then follows from work of Jannsen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Theorem 3.6]</cite> that the map <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g})\rightarrow H^{2*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g})\rightarrow H^{2*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is not injective for <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In our previous work about tautological classes on the Hurwitz space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, degree four covers <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> that factor through a lower genus curve presented a major difficulty. The primary example of this issue is when <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bielliptic</em>, where degree four covers <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> arise from the double cover <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow D</annotation></semantics></math> and any double cover <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is an elliptic curve.
A main challenge of this paper is therefore to prove that classes supported on the bielliptic locus of <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological.
Indeed, the bielliptic locus is the source of the first known example of a <em id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nontautological</em> algebraic class on <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>: in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, van Zelm proves that the fundamental class of the locus of bielliptic curves <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{12}\subset\mathcal{M}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{12}\subset\mathcal{M}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> is nontautological.
The techniques we develop for the bielliptic locus in genus <math id="S1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math>
also extend to genus <math id="S1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The fundamental class of the bielliptic locus <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{10}\subset\mathcal{M}_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{10}\subset\mathcal{M}_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological (hence equal to zero).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Overview of the proof</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our basic approach is to use the stratification of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> by <em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gonality</em>, the minimal degree of a map <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Precisely, let us define</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}:=\{[C]\in\mathcal{M}_{g}:C\text{ has a }g^{1}_{k}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> has a </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}:=\{[C]\in\mathcal{M}_{g}:C\text{ has a }g^{1}_{k}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is the locus of curves of gonality less than or equal to <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=7,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=7,8</annotation></semantics></math>, a general curve of genus <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> has gonality <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, so our stratification takes the form</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{2}_{g}\subset\mathcal{M}^{3}_{g}\subset\mathcal{M}^{4}_{g}\subset\mathcal{M}^{5}_{g}=\mathcal{M}_{g}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>5</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{2}_{g}\subset\mathcal{M}^{3}_{g}\subset\mathcal{M}^{4}_{g}\subset\mathcal{M}^{5}_{g}=\mathcal{M}_{g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In genus <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>, a general curve has gonality <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>, so we have one more stratum</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{2}_{9}\subset\mathcal{M}^{3}_{9}\subset\mathcal{M}^{4}_{9}\subset\mathcal{M}^{5}_{9}\subset\mathcal{M}_{9}^{6}=\mathcal{M}_{9}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>5</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>6</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{2}_{9}\subset\mathcal{M}^{3}_{9}\subset\mathcal{M}^{4}_{9}\subset\mathcal{M}^{5}_{9}\subset\mathcal{M}_{9}^{6}=\mathcal{M}_{9}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">It suffices to show for each <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> that all classes supported on <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological up to classes supported on <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, we must show that every class in <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math> pushes forward to a class in <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math> that is the restriction of a tautological class on <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We shall call a class on <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math> tautological if it is the restriction of a tautological class from <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases, each stratum <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes more complicated.
Our main contribution is a better understanding of the strata for <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4,5</annotation></semantics></math>, which was the main stumbling block in extending previous work on low genus curves.
We now explain our process in more detail, starting with the curves of lowest gonality and working upwards.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">(1) An easy start</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> showed that the fundamental class of any Brill–Noether locus of the expected dimension is tautological. In particular the fundamental class of <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological.
It is well known that <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{2})\cong\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{2})\cong\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. By a result of Patel-Vakil <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the restriction of <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\neq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\neq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using Faber’s result and the push-pull formula this establishes for <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\neq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\neq 3</annotation></semantics></math> that</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.E1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">all classes supported on <math id="S1.E1.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">(See Remark <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Remark 2.5. ‣ 2. Hurwitz Schemes and the Tautological Ring ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> for an alternative argument when <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>.)
Note that (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In (1) An easy start ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) already establishes that <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.
More generally, using the push-pull formula and Faber’s result, if the Chow ring of each locally closed stratum <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math> were generated by the restrictions of tautological classes for all <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we would be done. However, this is not the case for <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">(2) Why it must get harder</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4,5</annotation></semantics></math>,
the Chow ring of <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
is <em id="S1.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> in general generated by restrictions of tautological classes. By considering curves of bidegree <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,4)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}\times\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}\times\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can show that when <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, the map <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta:\mathcal{H}_{4,g}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta:\mathcal{H}_{4,g}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism away from loci of codimension <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> in both spaces. Therefore, the Picard rank conjecture, proved by Deopurkar–Patel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> for <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4,5</annotation></semantics></math>, shows that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim A^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{4}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{3})=\dim A^{1}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})=2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim A^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{4}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{3})=\dim A^{1}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})=2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Hence, the first Chow group of the locally closed stratum <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{4}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{4}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be generated by the restriction of <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The analogous result holds for <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, it is known that there exist classes supported on <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> that are not tautological in some genera:
van Zelm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> has shown that the fundamental class of the bielliptic locus <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{12}\subset\mathcal{M}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{12}\subset\mathcal{M}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.SSx2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> a tautological class. This makes the tetragonal locus (Section <a href="#S4" title="4. The Tetragonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) one of the most interesting parts, and it will, of course, require some special observations about genus <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> curves. (In Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Genus 
          
            5
          
         and 
          
            6 ‣ 4. The Tetragonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we also explain how to prove <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> using our techniques.) In Section <a href="#S4.SS7" title="4.7. The end of our luck: bielliptics in genus 
          
            10
          
         and beyond ‣ 4. The Tetragonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>, we discuss why our techniques cannot access the bielliptic locus when <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>. In the case <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=10</annotation></semantics></math>, we prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a>: the class of the bielliptic locus <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{10}\subset\mathcal{M}_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{10}\subset\mathcal{M}_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">(3) Using the Hurwitz space</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach is to study the Chow rings of the Hurwitz stacks <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{5,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{5,g}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing degree <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> covers, respectively, of the projective line. Let <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta:\mathcal{H}_{k,g}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta:\mathcal{H}_{k,g}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the forgetful map. The induced map <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1})\to\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1})\to\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and surjective, and thus induces a surjection on rational Chow groups.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, we showed that for <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4,5</annotation></semantics></math>, classes in the <em id="S1.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tautological ring of</em> <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}^{k-1}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}^{k-1}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> (see Definition <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Definition 2.2. ‣ 2. Hurwitz Schemes and the Tautological Ring ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>) push forward to tautological classes on <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}^{k-1}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}^{k-1}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 2.4. ‣ 2. Hurwitz Schemes and the Tautological Ring ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>).
This is a useful (and non-trivial) tool because there are tautological classes on the Hurwitz space which are <em id="S1.SSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> pullbacks of tautological classes on <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, we wish to show that the Chow rings of <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}^{k-1}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}^{k-1}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological for <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4,5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=7,8,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=7,8,9</annotation></semantics></math>. We succeed in proving this in each of these cases except <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4,g=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4,g=9</annotation></semantics></math>, where some additional special arguments are used. These arguments are carried out in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. The Tetragonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4</annotation></semantics></math> and Section <a href="#S5" title="5. The Pentagonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> for <math id="S1.SSx3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To accomplish this, we further stratify <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a cover <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}:=(\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{C}/\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}:=(\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{C}/\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\alpha}:=\ker(\Sym^{2}E_{\alpha}\to\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes 2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\alpha}:=\ker(\Sym^{2}E_{\alpha}\to\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes 2})</annotation></semantics></math>, which are vector bundles on <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
See Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ 2. Hurwitz Schemes and the Tautological Ring ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> for an elaboration of the properties of <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then stratify <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> by the pair of splitting types of <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each of these “pair splitting loci” has a nice description as a quotient stack (Lemmas <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 3.10. ‣ 3.1. Pair splitting loci on 
          
            
              
              H
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  4
                  ,
                  g ‣ 3. Splitting Loci ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Lemma 3.11. ‣ 3.2. Pair splitting loci on 
          
            
              
              H
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  5
                  ,
                  g ‣ 3. Splitting Loci ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>).
As a starting point, our previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> shows that the Chow ring of a union <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> of the several largest strata is generated by tautological classes (Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 2.9. ‣ 2.1. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ 2. Hurwitz Schemes and the Tautological Ring ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>). This result allows us to narrow down the possible sources of non-tautological classes: they all occur on the complement of <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>. Some “bad” pair splitting loci <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> remain outside
of <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> and not inside <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. These bad <math id="S1.SSx3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the main focus of this paper. Part of the difficulty of these strata is that they all occur in the “unexpected (pair) codimension” in the sense of Deopurkar–Patel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, Remark 4.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">(4) The key coincidence and work to be done.</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using universal degeneracy formulas from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, we show that the fundamental class of a single splitting locus (i.e. where <em id="S1.SSx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">one</em> of the two vector bundles has a given splitting type) is tautological if it occurs in the “expected codimension.”
Perhaps the most surprising part of the proof is the following coincidence (when <math id="S1.SSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,g)\neq(4,9)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,g)\neq(4,9)</annotation></semantics></math>): after excising strata contained in <math id="S1.SSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}^{k-1}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}^{k-1}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, every bad <math id="S1.SSx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be realized as a single splitting locus, and that single splitting locus occurs in the expected codimension (proofs of Lemmas <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 4.8. ‣ 4.4. Genus 
          
            7
          
         tetragonal curves ‣ 4. The Tetragonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>, <a href="#S4.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 4.9. ‣ 4.5. Genus 
          
            8
          
         tetragonal curves ‣ 4. The Tetragonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a>, <a href="#S5.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 5.7. ‣ 5.4. Genus 
          
            8 ‣ 5. The Pentagonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>, <a href="#S5.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 5.9. ‣ 5.5. Genus 
          
            9 ‣ 5. The Pentagonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a>). Hence, the fundamental class of the closure of each bad <math id="S1.SSx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological in <math id="S1.SSx4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}^{k-1}_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}^{k-1}_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math>. That these fundamental classes are tautological is a “coincidence of small numbers.” It in fact fails for <math id="S1.SSx4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SSx4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=12</annotation></semantics></math> by the result of van Zelm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then study the Chow rings of the locally closed strata <math id="S1.SSx4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using the description of <math id="S1.SSx4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as a quotient, we show that the Chow ring of each stratum <math id="S1.SSx4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\Sigma_{i}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\Sigma_{i}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{k-1}))</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by restrictions of tautological classes on <math id="S1.SSx4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> (Sections <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Chow rings of very unbalanced splitting strata ‣ 4. The Tetragonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> and <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Chow rings of very unbalanced splitting strata ‣ 5. The Pentagonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>). This last step requires a geometric understanding of the equations that define <math id="S1.SSx4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> inside the associated scroll <math id="S1.SSx4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>, and when a collection of equations of this type fail to define a smooth curve or produce a curve of gonality less than <math id="S1.SSx4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. These ideas do extend to arbitrary genus, unlike the results in the previous paragraph concerning fundamental classes. We state them as broadly as possible for arbitrary genera as they may be of future use.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">(5) The further work in genus <math id="S1.SSx5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S1.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As the genus increases, the luck with fundamental classes starts to run out and more subtle arguments are required.
In the case <math id="S1.SSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=9,k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=9,k=4</annotation></semantics></math>, we encounter two bad pair strata <math id="S1.SSx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that occur in unexpected codimension and cannot be realized as a single splitting locus. Although we do not compute their classes on <math id="S1.SSx5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,9}</annotation></semantics></math>, we still manage to show that their push forwards to <math id="S1.SSx5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{9}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{9}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{9}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{9}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological. For example, one of these problem strata corresponds to the locus of plane sextics with one double point (Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm14" title="Lemma 4.14. ‣ 4.6. Genus 
          
            9
          
         tetragonal curves ‣ 4. The Tetragonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.14</span></a>). The class of this locus is tautological on <math id="S1.SSx5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{9}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{9}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{9}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{9}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> because it is a Brill–Noether locus of the expected codimension. We then show that the Chow ring of this stratum is generated by the pullback of <math id="S1.SSx5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a stronger statement than being generated by restrictions of tautological classes on <math id="S1.SSx5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,9}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the push-pull formula, the push forward of every class supported on this stratum is tautological on <math id="S1.SSx5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{9}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{9}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{9}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{9}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> (though we remain unsure if they are tautological upstairs on <math id="S1.SSx5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,9}</annotation></semantics></math>). We deal with the other problem stratum by showing that its union with the bielliptic locus <math id="S1.SSx5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{B}_{9})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{B}_{9})</annotation></semantics></math> has tautological fundamental class on <math id="S1.SSx5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,9}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{9}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,9}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{9}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> and a trick explained in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.6. Genus 
          
            9
          
         tetragonal curves ‣ 4. The Tetragonal Locus ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In genus <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>, we must also deal with curves of gonality <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>. The approach we take to these curves in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. The General Genus 9 Curve ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> is quite different from the approach taken to curves of gonality <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> and below because there is no uniform description for degree <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> covers in terms of associated vector bundles.
Instead, using results of Mukai <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, we realize <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{9}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{9}^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>5</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{9}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{9}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> (up to a <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> gerbe) as a global quotient of an open subvariety of a Grassmannian by <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SP_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SP_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>. The tautological subbundle on the resulting Grassmann bundle over <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BSp_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BSp</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BSp_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Hodge bundle (up to possibly twisting by a line bundle, see Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 6.9. ‣ 6. The General Genus 9 Curve ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.9</span></a>). It then remains to see that Chern classes of the rank <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to this quotient are tautological.
We see this by proving that the rank <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym^{2}\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym^{2}\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is the bundle of <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21</annotation></semantics></math> quadrics that cut out a canonical genus <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> curve (Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 6.10. ‣ 6. The General Genus 9 Curve ‣ The Chow Rings of the Moduli Spaces of Curves of Genus 7, 8, and 9" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.10</span></a>). From this, it follows that the Chern classes of <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym^{2}\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym^{2}\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological, and, using the splitting principle, the Chern classes of <math id="S1.SSx5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> are seen to be tautological as well.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Notations and conventions.</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All schemes in this paper are taken over a fixed algebraically closed field of characteristic <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;5</annotation></semantics></math>. All Chow rings are taken with rational coefficients. We use the subspace convention for projective bundles and Grassmann bundles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgments</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to thank Jarod Alper, Andrea Di Lorenzo, Giovanni Inchiostro, Elham Izadi, Aaron Landesman, and Ravi Vakil for helpful conversations. We thank Gregor Botero and Maxwell da Paixão de Jesus Santos for their correspondence. We are also grateful to Johannes Schmitt and Jason van Zelm for their advice and help on computing the tautological rings with admcycles, and to David Holmes and Johannes Schmitt for correcting a miscalculation in an earlier version of the presentation of the tautological rings. In addition, we thank Rahul Pandharipande, Dan Petersen, and Burt Totaro for their comments on an early draft of this article. We thank the referee for insightful comments that helped improve this paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Hurwitz Schemes and the Tautological Ring</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to study the loci <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{4}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{4}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{5}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>5</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{5}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, we will study the Hurwitz stacks <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{5,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{5,g}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing degree <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> covers, respectively, of the projective line.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The unparametrized Hurwitz stack <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stack whose objects over a scheme <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are of the form <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C\rightarrow P\rightarrow S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C\rightarrow P\rightarrow S)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-fibration, <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow P</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite flat finitely presented morphism of constant degree <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and the composition <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of smooth genus <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Hurwitz stack <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a universal diagram</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
